# Layer 2 for the kernels this machine's GPU cannot run (knife TA), the
# twenty-fifth of these programs: one `!Gpu` program runs each kernel
# scripts/tile-golden pins under `with_gpu_real` against a GPU whose
# architecture is high enough for it, and under `with_gpu_fake` against
# hand-written host references.
#
#   arch_diff <kernel> <cubin> [<kernel> <cubin> ...]
#
# THE ARGUMENTS ARE PAIRS and not a fixed list, which is the one thing this
# family's command line does that the other twenty-four do not. Their
# kernels either all run on a machine or none of them do, so a positional
# list says everything; here the runnable set is a function of the machine,
# and on the H200 ledger it is two of the five. run.sh passes the pairs it
# could assemble and names the rest in the ledger line.
#
# EVERY OTHER FAMILY HERE RUNS EVERYWHERE THIS SCRIPT RUNS. This one does
# not, and that is its whole reason for existing: `tileiras` refuses these
# element formats below a floor that no card in this repository's own
# machine reaches, so their rows in scripts/tileir-features/types.txt and
# features.txt carried an `architecture` exemption from knife T3 until a GPU
# cluster made them reachable. run.sh runs this family only on a ledger
# whose gpu-name is at or above each kernel's floor and names the skipped
# ones in the ledger line otherwise, because a SKIP is not a pass and the
# record has to be able to tell them apart.
#
# THE FLOORS ARE MEASURED, one `tileiras --gpu-name` at a time, and knife TA
# had to correct the earlier reading to write them down:
#
#   `addf` over bf16      refused at sm_80, sm_86 and sm_87, accepted from
#                         sm_89 (knife T13's measurement, and the lowest
#                         floor here)
#   f8E4M3FN, f8E5M2      refused at sm_86 and sm_89, ACCEPTED AT sm_90
#   f8E8M0FNU, f4E2M1FN   refused at sm_86, sm_89 and sm_90, accepted at sm_100
#   mmaf_scaled           sm_100, and by the scale rather than the operands:
#                         at sm_90 it is the f8E8M0FNU scale that is refused
#
# So the fp8 floor is not one floor. Knives T3 and T9 recorded "sm_86 and
# sm_89 refuse, sm_100 accepts" and never tried the step between; the H200
# ledger runs three of these six and skips three, which is the shape that
# says the two halves of fp8 are two different walls.
#
# WHAT ONLY A DEVICE CAN SAY, and what this program was written to ask:
#
#   * WHAT AN fp8 BIT PATTERN IS WORTH. Four exponent bits and three
#     significand ones for f8E4M3FN, five and two for f8E5M2; the storage
#     segment of each kernel reads a buffer of the format and widens it, so
#     what it asks is the layout, and the conversion segment asks where the
#     rounding boundaries are.
#   * WHERE f8E4M3FN's TOP END IS. The format has no infinity: the all-ones
#     exponent is a normal one and only the all-ones significand under it is
#     NaN, so the largest finite value is 448 and there is nothing above it
#     to overflow to. std/narrow's `round_f8e4m3fn` says in its own doc
#     comment that the overflow answer was A CHOICE IT COULD NOT MEASURE.
#     The corpus below puts 464 (the midpoint, which ties down to 448), 480
#     and an infinity on named lanes, so this run is that measurement.
#   * WHETHER f8E8M0FNU IS AN EXPONENT AND NOTHING ELSE. No sign, no
#     significand, no zero, no infinity: 255 powers of two and NaN. Its
#     `ftof` takes only `zero` or `positive_inf` rounding, never the
#     `nearest_even` every other conversion here writes, so the answer for
#     an ordinary number is the largest power of two at or below it. The
#     corpus carries the negatives and the zero that have no value in the
#     format at all, which std/narrow answers NaN for and which no device
#     had ever been asked.
#   * WHICH OF SIXTEEN VALUES A NIBBLE IS. f4E2M1FN has no buffer
#     (`element_bytes` refuses it), so dtype_e2m1 ships its lanes as i32
#     words and `pack` / `unpack` reach the nibbles. Segment 0 is therefore
#     the ENCODING -- which of the sixteen finite values each pattern names
#     -- and segment 1 is the rounding, whose saturation at 6 is the other
#     unmeasured choice std/narrow records.
#   * WHETHER AN ATOMIC ADD CAN BE bf16. `atomic_red_view_tko`'s `addf` over
#     a BF16 tile is the one thing in the whole view family that Ops.td
#     gates on an architecture, and it gates it IN PROSE rather than in a
#     verifier. Knife T13 measured the gate (sm_80, sm_86 and sm_87 refuse,
#     sm_89 and up accept, so the prose's "from Hopper" is a generation
#     late) and could go no further, because this repository's card is on
#     the wrong side of it. Eight tile blocks reduce into sixteen bins in no
#     order at all, and what a device answers for that is what this asks.
#   * WHETHER A SCALE SCALES A BLOCK. `mmaf_scaled` broadcasts one scale
#     element over a run of V elements along K, and V is not an operand and
#     not an attribute: it is the ratio of two shapes. A device that
#     broadcast the scale over the whole of K, or applied it per element,
#     would answer a different matrix, and the corpus below makes the two
#     scale blocks differ so that it does.
#
# THE TIER IS EXACT for all five, and by construction rather than by luck.
# The four conversion kernels have no reduction in them at all. The matrix
# product does, and its fold order over K is not specified anywhere, so the
# corpus keeps every operand a small whole number and every scale a power of
# two between 2^-2 and 2^3: every product and every partial sum is a whole
# number far inside 2^24, where f32 addition is exact and therefore
# associative, and the fold order cannot be read off the answer.
use std/io
use std/io.{Exit, with_fs_real}
use std/gpu
use std/gpu.{Gpu, with_gpu_fake, with_gpu_real, WideRefFn, last_out, dtype_convert_ref,
  dtype_e2m1_ref, mmaf_scaled_ref, view_atomic_bf16_ref, wrap_i32, nibble_word }
use std/list
use std/map
use std/narrow

## The sentinel every output buffer holds before a launch: a value no kernel
## here computes, so a lane that still has it is a lane nothing wrote.
pub const SENTINEL: Float = 0.0 - 12345.0

## The shapes the kernels were recorded with. These must agree with
## scripts/tile-golden/kernels.dawn: DT_N and DT_TILE are the four
## conversion kernels' segment length and tile width (the fp4 one has the
## same two and a narrower input, sixteen i32 words to a block), and the
## four MMS_ constants are the block-scaled product's.
pub const DT_N: Int = 512
pub const DT_TILE: Int = 128
pub const E2M1_WORDS: Int = 16
pub const MMS_M: Int = 32
pub const MMS_K: Int = 64
pub const MMS_N: Int = 32
pub const MMS_V: Int = 32
pub const VA_BINS: Int = 16
pub const VA_TILE: Int = 64
pub const VA_N: Int = 500

## The lanes past the answer in every output buffer: a write past the end
## leaves the sentinel behind and is seen.
const TAIL: Int = 8

## The number of differing lanes the transcript prints. One is enough to
## know that a run is red and not enough to know WHY when the disagreement
## is a handful of named corner lanes, which is what half of this corpus is.
const SHOWN_DIFFERENCES: Int = 12

type Trace = {
  alloc: String,
  upload: String,
  launch: String,
  sync: String,
  download: String,
  result: Option[List[Float]],
  note: String
}

fn kind[T](r: Result[T, ForeignError]) -> String = match r {
  Ok(_) -> "ok"
  Err(e) -> e.kind
}

fn message[T](r: Result[T, ForeignError]) -> String = match r {
  Ok(_) -> ""
  Err(e) -> e.message
}

fn blank() -> Trace =
  Trace { alloc: "-", upload: "-", launch: "-", sync: "-", download: "-", result: None, note: "" }

# Allocate and fill one buffer per (format, contents) pair, in order.
# Written as recursion rather than a `for` with `?` in it, for the reason
# gath_diff gives (dawn-lang issue #68).
fn alloc_all(bufs: List[(String, List[Float])], i: Int, acc: List[Int]) -> Result[List[Int], ForeignError] !Gpu =
  if i >= len(bufs) {
    Ok(acc)
  } else {
    let (dt, data) = bufs[i]
    match gpu.gpu_alloc(dt, len(data)) {
      Ok(h) -> alloc_all(bufs, i + 1, acc ++ [h])
      Err(e) -> Err(e)
    }
  }

fn upload_all(hs: List[Int], bufs: List[(String, List[Float])], i: Int) -> Result[Unit, ForeignError] !Gpu =
  if i >= len(hs) {
    Ok(())
  } else {
    let (_dt, data) = bufs[i]
    match gpu.gpu_upload(hs[i], data) {
      Ok(_) -> upload_all(hs, bufs, i + 1)
      Err(e) -> Err(e)
    }
  }

fn release(hs: List[Int]) -> Unit !Gpu = {
  for h in hs {
    let _ = gpu.gpu_free(h)
  }
}

## One kernel over `bufs` (each a format and its initial contents) on a
## `gx` by 1 by 1 grid, one stage at a time, stopping at the first refusal
## and saying which stage it was. The LAST buffer is the output, and the
## one this reads back.
fn stages(kernel: String, gx: Int, bufs: List[(String, List[Float])]) -> Trace !Gpu =
  match alloc_all(bufs, 0, []) {
    Err(e) -> Trace { ..blank(), alloc: e.kind, note: e.message }
    Ok(hs) -> {
      let t0 = Trace { ..blank(), alloc: "ok" }
      match upload_all(hs, bufs, 0) {
        Err(e) -> {
          release(hs)
          Trace { ..t0, upload: e.kind, note: e.message }
        }
        Ok(_) -> {
          let t1 = Trace { ..t0, upload: "ok" }
          match gpu.gpu_launch(kernel, gx, 1, 1, hs) {
            Err(e) -> {
              release(hs)
              Trace { ..t1, launch: e.kind, note: e.message }
            }
            Ok(_) -> {
              let s = gpu.gpu_sync()
              let r = gpu.gpu_download(hs[len(hs) - 1])
              release(hs)
              let value = match r {
                Ok(v) -> Some(v)
                Err(_) -> None
              }
              Trace {
                ..t1,
                launch: "ok",
                sync: kind(s),
                download: kind(r),
                result: value,
                note: message(s) ++ message(r)
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

## Where the real device stopped, as `<kind>@<stage>`, or "" if it did not.
fn stopped(t: Trace) -> String =
  if t.alloc != "ok" {
    t.alloc ++ "@alloc"
  } else if t.upload != "ok" {
    t.upload ++ "@upload"
  } else if t.launch != "ok" {
    t.launch ++ "@launch"
  } else if t.sync != "ok" {
    t.sync ++ "@sync"
  } else if t.download != "ok" {
    t.download ++ "@download"
  } else {
    ""
  }

fn rendered(o: Option[List[Float]]) -> String = match o {
  Some(v) -> to_string(v)
  None -> "-"
}

## The verdict for one kernel. Every kernel here is EXACT tier, so there is
## no tolerance arm: the two renderings are equal or they are not.
fn verdict(fake: Trace, real: Trace) -> String = {
  let stop = stopped(real)
  if stop != "" {
    "blocked:" ++ stop
  } else if rendered(real.result) == rendered(fake.result) {
    "identical:exact"
  } else {
    "differ:result"
  }
}

## EVERY differing lane, up to `SHOWN_DIFFERENCES` of them, as segment and
## lane with both answers. The other families here print the FIRST
## difference and no more, and that is the right amount when a corpus is a
## sweep. This one is half named corner lanes -- an overflow midpoint, an
## infinity, a negative into a format with no sign -- and std/narrow's own
## comments say two of its answers were choices nobody could measure. One
## line per corner turns one run of this program into the whole
## measurement instead of the first disagreement in it.
fn differences(real: Trace, fake: Trace, n: Int) -> String =
  match (real.result, fake.result) {
    (Some(a), Some(b)) ->
      if len(a) != len(b) {
        "lengths ${len(a)} and ${len(b)}"
      } else {
        let bad = list.filter(range(0, len(a)), i => to_string(a[i]) != to_string(b[i]))
        if len(bad) == 0 {
          "-"
        } else {
          let shown = list.take(bad, SHOWN_DIFFERENCES)
          let more = if len(bad) > SHOWN_DIFFERENCES {
            " (+${len(bad) - SHOWN_DIFFERENCES} more)"
          } else {
            ""
          }
          list.fold(shown, "${len(bad)} lanes:", (acc, i) =>
              acc ++ " [seg ${i / n} lane ${i % n} device ${a[i]} host ${b[i]}]") ++ more
        }
      }
    _ -> "-"
  }

fn pow2(k: Int) -> Float = {
  var q = 1.0
  var i = k
  while i > 0 {
    q = q * 2.0
    i = i - 1
  }
  while i < 0 {
    q = q * 0.5
    i = i + 1
  }
  q
}

fn magnitude(v: Float) -> Float = if v < 0.0 { 0.0 - v } else { v }

# ---- the corpus ----

fn sentinel_buffer(n: Int) -> List[Float] = list.map(range(0, n), _i => SENTINEL)

## The f8E4M3FN storage corpus: the format's own boundaries on the first
## lanes and an ordinary sweep after them. `upload` rounds every lane into
## the format, so segment 0 answers the buffer rather than these values; the
## pinned lanes are here so that the buffer holds a zero, a negative zero,
## the smallest subnormal, the largest finite value and its negation.
fn e4m3_x() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        0.0
      } else if i == 1 {
        0.0 - 0.0
      } else if i == 2 {
        pow2(0 - 9)
      } else if i == 3 {
        pow2(0 - 6)
      } else if i == 4 {
        448.0
      } else if i == 5 {
        0.0 - 448.0
      } else {
        to_float(i - 256) * 0.0123456789
      })

## The f64 the second segment converts into f8E4M3FN and back. The first
## eight lanes are the whole of what the format's top end can be asked, and
## the answers std/narrow gives them are the two it says it could not
## measure: 464 is the midpoint above 448 and ties DOWN to it because 448's
## significand is even, and anything above answers NaN because the format
## has nothing else left.
fn e4m3_y() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        1.0 + pow2(0 - 4)
      } else if i == 1 {
        1.0 + 3.0 * pow2(0 - 4)
      } else if i == 2 {
        448.0
      } else if i == 3 {
        464.0
      } else if i == 4 {
        480.0
      } else if i == 5 {
        0.0 - 480.0
      } else if i == 6 {
        1.0 / 0.0
      } else if i == 7 {
        0.0 / 0.0
      } else if i == 8 {
        pow2(0 - 11)
      } else {
        to_float(i - 256) * 0.1 + 1.0 / 3.0
      })

## The f8E5M2 storage corpus: five exponent bits and two significand ones,
## so this format DOES have an infinity and its largest finite value is
## 57344. The pinned lanes are the same five questions as f8E4M3FN's, asked
## of the other split of the byte.
fn e5m2_x() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        0.0
      } else if i == 1 {
        0.0 - 0.0
      } else if i == 2 {
        pow2(0 - 16)
      } else if i == 3 {
        pow2(0 - 14)
      } else if i == 4 {
        57344.0
      } else if i == 5 {
        0.0 - 57344.0
      } else {
        to_float(i - 256) * 0.0123456789
      })

## The f64 the f8E5M2 conversion segment rounds. This format DOES have an
## infinity, so its top end is an ordinary IEEE overflow rather than the
## choice f8E4M3FN's is: 61440 is the midpoint above the largest finite
## value and ties to even, which here is the infinity.
fn e5m2_y() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        1.0 + pow2(0 - 3)
      } else if i == 1 {
        1.0 + 3.0 * pow2(0 - 3)
      } else if i == 2 {
        57344.0
      } else if i == 3 {
        1.0e30
      } else if i == 4 {
        0.0 - 1.0e30
      } else if i == 5 {
        1.0 / 0.0
      } else if i == 6 {
        0.0 / 0.0
      } else if i == 7 {
        pow2(0 - 18)
      } else if i == 8 {
        pow2(0 - 17)
      } else {
        to_float(i - 256) * 0.1 + 1.0 / 3.0
      })

## The f8E8M0FNU storage corpus: the format is the exponent alone, so every
## lane a buffer of it can hold is a power of two and `upload` rounds each
## of these DOWN to one. The pinned lanes are the two ends of the range.
fn e8m0_x() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        pow2(0 - 127)
      } else if i == 1 {
        pow2(127)
      } else if i == 2 {
        1.0
      } else {
        to_float(i) * 0.75
      })

## The f64 the second segment takes into f8E8M0FNU and back, THROUGH
## BINARY32 because the kernel does. The first lanes are the values the
## format has no encoding for at all -- zero, a negative, an infinity and a
## NaN -- which std/narrow answers NaN for and which nothing had ever
## asked a device.
fn e8m0_y() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        1.0
      } else if i == 1 {
        1.99
      } else if i == 2 {
        pow2(127)
      } else if i == 3 {
        pow2(0 - 127)
      } else if i == 4 {
        0.0
      } else if i == 5 {
        0.0 - 1.0
      } else if i == 6 {
        1.0 / 0.0
      } else if i == 7 {
        0.0 / 0.0
      } else if i == 8 {
        pow2(0 - 140)
      } else if i == 9 {
        pow2(140)
      } else {
        to_float(i) * 0.5 + 0.25
      })

## The fp4 storage corpus, in i32 words: eight nibbles to a word, and the
## sweep is over the NIBBLES. Word 0 puts nibbles 0 to 7 on lanes 0 to 7 and
## word 1 puts 8 to 15, so the first two words are the sixteen encodings in
## order and every later word walks them at another stride.
fn e2m1_x() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N / 8), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        nibble_word(k => k)
      } else if i == 1 {
        nibble_word(k => k + 8)
      } else {
        nibble_word(k => (5 * i + 3 * k + 1) % 16)
      })

## The f64 the fp4 conversion segment rounds. The format holds sixteen
## values and nothing else, so the pinned lanes are its two unmeasured
## answers: a magnitude above 6 saturates rather than overflowing, and a NaN
## goes to +0 because there is no NaN encoding to go to.
fn e2m1_y() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, DT_N), i =>
      if i == 0 {
        1.25
      } else if i == 1 {
        1.75
      } else if i == 2 {
        5.0
      } else if i == 3 {
        6.5
      } else if i == 4 {
        1.0e30
      } else if i == 5 {
        0.0 - 1.0e30
      } else if i == 6 {
        0.25
      } else if i == 7 {
        0.75
      } else {
        to_float(i % 29) * 0.25 - 3.5
      })

## The block-scaled product's four inputs. The operands are small whole
## numbers and the scales are powers of two, which is what keeps the tier
## exact: every product is a whole number, every partial sum is far inside
## 2^24, and the fold order over K cannot be read off the answer.
##
## THE TWO SCALE BLOCKS DIFFER, and that is the point rather than the
## corpus being varied. K is 64 and V is 32, so each row of `sa` is two
## numbers and each column of `sb` is two: a device that broadcast one
## scale over the whole of K, or applied a scale per element, answers a
## different matrix, and one that ignored the scales entirely answers the
## unscaled product.
fn mms_a() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, MMS_M * MMS_K), t => to_float((t % 7) - 3))

fn mms_b() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, MMS_K * MMS_N), t => to_float((t % 5) - 2))

fn mms_sa() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, MMS_M * (MMS_K / MMS_V)), t => pow2(t % 4 - 2))

fn mms_sb() -> List[Float] =
  list.map(range(0, (MMS_K / MMS_V) * MMS_N), t => pow2((t + 1) % 4 - 1))

## The bf16 atomic reduction's input: VA_N lanes, each naming one of the
## VA_BINS bins. The stride is coprime with the bin count so that every bin
## is hit, and no bin comes near 256, which is where whole numbers stop
## being exact in bf16 and the order eight blocks arrive in would start to
## show.
fn va_x() -> List[Float] = list.map(range(0, VA_N), i => to_float((i * 7 + 3) % VA_BINS))

## The blocks `view_atomic_bf16` is launched with: the same expression
## scripts/tile-golden/kernels.dawn's `va_blocks` is, and the last one is
## the partial block the kernel masks.
fn va_blocks() -> Int = VA_N / VA_TILE + 1

# ---- the cases ----

## Every kernel this program knows, in the order the corpus functions below
## are written. A command line names a subset of these and gives each one a
## cubin.
pub fn case_name(i: Int) -> String =
  ["dtype_e4m3", "dtype_e5m2", "dtype_e8m0", "dtype_e2m1", "mmaf_scaled_e4m3",
    "view_atomic_bf16"][i]

pub const CASES: Int = 6

## The index of the kernel called `name`, or -1 for a name this program does
## not know.
fn case_index(name: String) -> Int =
  match list.find(range(0, CASES), i => case_name(i) == name) {
    Some(i) -> i
    None -> 0 - 1
  }

## The number of output segments each kernel writes.
fn case_segments(i: Int) -> Int = if i >= 4 { 1 } else { 2 }

## The lanes in one segment of each kernel's output.
fn case_lanes(i: Int) -> Int =
  if i == 4 {
    MMS_M * MMS_N
  } else if i == 5 {
    VA_BINS
  } else {
    DT_N
  }

## The blocks each kernel is launched with. The block-scaled product names
## no block id at all and runs on one.
fn case_grid(i: Int) -> Int =
  if i == 4 {
    1
  } else if i == 5 {
    va_blocks()
  } else {
    DT_N / DT_TILE
  }

## The buffers each kernel is handed: a FORMAT and the initial contents, in
## argument order. Every output buffer is LONGER than the answer, so the
## sentinel in its tail says whether a write went past the end.
fn case_bufs(i: Int) -> List[(String, List[Float])] = {
  let out = sentinel_buffer(case_segments(i) * case_lanes(i) + TAIL)
  if i == 0 {
    [("f8E4M3FN", e4m3_x()), ("f64", e4m3_y()), ("f64", out)]
  } else if i == 1 {
    [("f8E5M2", e5m2_x()), ("f64", e5m2_y()), ("f64", out)]
  } else if i == 2 {
    [("f8E8M0FNU", e8m0_x()), ("f64", e8m0_y()), ("f64", out)]
  } else if i == 3 {
    [("i32", e2m1_x()), ("f64", e2m1_y()), ("f64", out)]
  } else if i == 4 {
    [("f8E4M3FN", mms_a()), ("f8E4M3FN", mms_b()), ("f8E8M0FNU", mms_sa()),
      ("f8E8M0FNU", mms_sb()), ("f64", out)]
  } else {
    [("i32", va_x()), ("bf16", out)]
  }
}

## The reference table the fake device answers with, for one case.
fn case_kernels(i: Int) -> Map[String, (Int, WideRefFn)] =
  if i == 0 {
    map.from([("dtype_e4m3", (3, last_out((dts, ins) => dtype_convert_ref(DT_N, "f8E4M3FN", dts, ins))))])
  } else if i == 1 {
    map.from([("dtype_e5m2", (3, last_out((dts, ins) => dtype_convert_ref(DT_N, "f8E5M2", dts, ins))))])
  } else if i == 2 {
    map.from([("dtype_e8m0", (3, last_out((dts, ins) => dtype_convert_ref(DT_N, "f8E8M0FNU", dts, ins))))])
  } else if i == 3 {
    map.from([("dtype_e2m1", (3, last_out((dts, ins) => dtype_e2m1_ref(DT_N, dts, ins))))])
  } else if i == 4 {
    map.from([("mmaf_scaled_e4m3",
      (5, last_out((dts, ins) => mmaf_scaled_ref(MMS_M, MMS_K, MMS_N, MMS_V, dts, ins))))])
  } else {
    map.from([("view_atomic_bf16",
      (2, last_out((dts, ins) => view_atomic_bf16_ref(VA_N, VA_BINS, VA_TILE, dts, ins))))])
  }

## The lanes of each corpus that matter, counted. run.sh holds every one of
## these above zero, which is what makes the claims in the header
## measurements rather than intentions: a conversion corpus already on the
## format's grid would let a truncating `ftof` pass for a rounding one, and
## a scale corpus whose two blocks agreed would let a device that ignored
## the block size pass for one that honours it.
fn shape_of(i: Int) -> String = {
  let idx = range(0, DT_N)
  if i == 5 {
    let x = va_x()
    let counts = list.map(range(0, VA_BINS), j =>
        len(list.filter(range(0, VA_N), t => to_int(x[t]) == j)))
    let hit = list.filter(counts, c => c > 0)
    let biggest = list.fold(counts, 0, (acc, c) => if c > acc { c } else { acc })
    "bins_hit=${len(hit)} blocks=${va_blocks()} max_count=${biggest}"
      ++ " exact_in_bf16=${if biggest < 256 { 1 } else { 0 }}"
  } else if i == 4 {
    let sa = mms_sa()
    let sb = mms_sb()
    let rows = range(0, MMS_M)
    let sk = MMS_K / MMS_V
    let split_a = list.filter(rows, r =>
        len(list.filter(range(1, sk), q => sa[r * sk + q] != sa[r * sk])) > 0)
    let split_b = list.filter(range(0, MMS_N), c =>
        len(list.filter(range(1, sk), q => sb[q * MMS_N + c] != sb[c])) > 0)
    let a = mms_a()
    let b = mms_b()
    let bound = list.fold(range(0, MMS_M * MMS_N), 0.0, (acc, t) => {
      let r = t / MMS_N
      let c = t % MMS_N
      let s = list.fold(range(0, MMS_K), 0.0, (q, k) =>
          q + magnitude(a[r * MMS_K + k] * b[k * MMS_N + c]) * 8.0)
      if s > acc { s } else { acc }
    })
    "scale_blocks=${sk} rows_with_split_scale=${len(split_a)}"
      ++ " cols_with_split_scale=${len(split_b)} bound_inside_2p24=${if bound < 16777216.0 { 1 } else { 0 }}"
  } else if i == 3 {
    let x = e2m1_x()
    let y = e2m1_y()
    let lanes = range(0, DT_N)
    let seen = list.filter(range(0, 16), v =>
        len(list.filter(lanes, j =>
            narrow.f4e2m1_bits(narrow.f4e2m1_of_bits(gpu.nibble_at(wrap_i32(to_int(x[j / 8])), j % 8))) == v)) > 0)
    let off = list.filter(lanes, j => narrow.round_f4e2m1(y[j]) != y[j])
    let saturated = list.filter(lanes, j => magnitude(y[j]) > 6.0)
    "encodings=${len(seen)} off_grid=${len(off)} saturating=${len(saturated)}"
  } else {
    let dtype = ["f8E4M3FN", "f8E5M2", "f8E8M0FNU"][i]
    let x = [e4m3_x(), e5m2_x(), e8m0_x()][i]
    let y = [e4m3_y(), e5m2_y(), e8m0_y()][i]
    let biggest = [448.0, 57344.0, pow2(127)][i]
    let off = list.filter(idx, j => gpu.round_to(dtype, y[j]) != y[j])
    let stored_off = list.filter(idx, j => gpu.round_to(dtype, x[j]) != x[j])
    let nan = list.filter(idx, j => {
      let v = gpu.round_to(dtype, y[j])
      v != v
    })
    let beyond = list.filter(idx, j => magnitude(y[j]) > biggest)
    "off_grid=${len(off)} rounded_on_upload=${len(stored_off)} host_nan=${len(nan)}"
      ++ " beyond_max=${len(beyond)}"
  }
}

## A vector as a line: its length, its first lanes and its last ones.
fn digest(v: List[Float]) -> String =
  if len(v) <= 8 {
    to_string(v)
  } else {
    "len=${len(v)} head=${to_string(list.take(v, 3))} tail=${to_string(list.drop(v, len(v) - 3))}"
  }

fn shown(o: Option[List[Float]]) -> String = match o {
  Some(v) -> digest(v)
  None -> "-"
}

fn buffers_of(i: Int) -> String = {
  let bufs = case_bufs(i)
  list.fold(bufs, "", (acc, b) => {
    let (dt, data) = b
    let sep = if acc == "" { "" } else { "," }
    acc ++ sep ++ dt ++ "[" ++ to_string(len(data)) ++ "]"
  })
}

fn read_cubin(path: String) -> Bytes !Exit !io = match with_fs_real(() => io.read_bytes(path)) {
  Ok(b) -> b
  Err(e) -> {
    io.eprintln("arch_diff: cannot read ${path}: ${e.message}")
    io.exit(2)
    bytes_of_nothing()
  }
}

fn usage() -> Unit !Exit !io = {
  io.eprintln("usage: arch_diff <kernel> <cubin> [<kernel> <cubin> ...], where <kernel> is one of"
      ++ " dtype_e4m3 dtype_e5m2 dtype_e8m0 dtype_e2m1 mmaf_scaled_e4m3 view_atomic_bf16")
  io.exit(2)
}

pub fn main() -> Unit !io = io.with_exit_real(() => {
  let paths = args()
  let count = len(paths) / 2
  if count == 0 || len(paths) != 2 * count { usage() } else { () }
  var cubins: Map[String, Bytes] = map.empty()
  var order: List[Int] = []
  for j in range(0, count) {
    let name = paths[2 * j]
    let i = case_index(name)
    if i < 0 {
      io.eprintln("arch_diff: no such kernel: ${name}")
      io.exit(2)
    } else {
      ()
    }
    cubins = map.insert(cubins, name, read_cubin(paths[2 * j + 1]))
    order = order ++ [i]
  }
  var all_agreed = true
  var blocked = ""
  var any_differ = false
  var exact_count = 0
  var ran = ""
  for i in order {
    let name = case_name(i)
    let bufs = case_bufs(i)
    let gx = case_grid(i)
    let refs = case_kernels(i)
    let fake = with_gpu_fake(refs, () => stages(name, gx, bufs))
    let real = with_gpu_real(cubins, () => stages(name, gx, bufs))
    let v = verdict(fake, real)
    io.println("kernel ${name} tier=exact bufs=${buffers_of(i)} grid=(${gx}, 1, 1)"
        ++ " segments=${case_segments(i)}")
    io.println("  index ${shape_of(i)}")
    io.println("  fake  launch=${fake.launch} result=${shown(fake.result)}")
    io.println("  real  alloc=${real.alloc} upload=${real.upload} launch=${real.launch}"
        ++ " sync=${real.sync} download=${real.download} result=${shown(real.result)}")
    io.println("  diffs ${differences(real, fake, case_lanes(i))}")
    if real.note != "" { io.println("  note  ${real.note}") } else { () }
    io.println("  verdict ${v}")
    if v == "identical:exact" {
      exact_count = exact_count + 1
      ran = if ran == "" { name } else { ran ++ "," ++ name }
    } else {
      ()
    }
    if v != "identical:exact" { all_agreed = false } else { () }
    if v == "differ:result" { any_differ = true } else { () }
    if blocked == "" && not any_differ && v != "identical:exact" { blocked = v } else { () }
  }
  io.println("ran ${if ran == "" { "-" } else { ran }}")
  io.println("tiers exact=${exact_count} tolerance=0")
  if all_agreed {
    io.println("tile-gpu-diff: pass")
  } else if any_differ {
    io.println("tile-gpu-diff: fail")
    io.exit(1)
  } else {
    io.println("tile-gpu-diff: ${blocked}")
  }
})

# `io.exit` is typed Unit, so the branch that cannot continue still owes a
# Bytes; this is never reached.
fn bytes_of_nothing() -> Bytes = panic("unreachable: exit did not exit")
